+
+DST-zone generators sample UTC, `America/New_York`, `Europe/Paris`,
+`Australia/Lord_Howe` (30-minute DST), `Asia/Kathmandu` (+05:45),
+`Pacific/Apia` (date-line change), `America/Sao_Paulo`, plus arbitrary
+whole-minute fixed offsets. Timestamps/dates/times/datetimes/spans/offsets
+use hegel's typed jiff generators at full range.
+
+## Test results
+
+`cargo test -p jiff --lib`: **513 passed, 3 failed.** The integration test
+target (`cargo test -p jiff --test integration`) passes: 215/215.
+
+- 31 of the 32 hegel tests pass, at the default 100 cases and in an
+  exploratory 10x (1000-case) run.
+- `span::tests::prop_display_fromstr_roundtrip_fieldwise` **fails** — kept
+  failing deliberately; suspected genuine library bug (below).
+- Liveness was verified by temporarily inverting an assertion
+  (`timestamp::tests::prop_display_fromstr_roundtrip`) and observing a
+  shrunk counterexample (`1970-01-01T00:00:00Z`), then reverting.
+- Two additional failures are **pre-existing and unrelated to this work**
+  (they fail on a pristine checkout in this environment):
+  `fmt::temporal::printer::tests::print_zoned` and
+  `zoned::tests::zoned_with_time_dst_us_eastern` both fail because this
+  container's `/usr/share/zoneinfo` lacks the legacy "backward" links
+  (`US/Eastern`, `America/Argentina/ComodRivadavia`). This is an
+  environment/tzdb-packaging issue, not a code bug.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+**Parsing a negative duration whose only non-zero component is fractional
+loses the sign.**
+
+Minimal counterexample (hegel shrank to the 1ns case):
+
+```rust
+"-PT0.000000001S".parse::<Span>()          // => Ok(1ns)      expected -1ns
+"-PT0.5S".parse::<Span>()                  // => Ok(500ms)    expected -500ms
+"-PT0.5S".parse::<SignedDuration>()        // => Ok(500ms)    expected -500ms
+"0.5s ago".parse::<Span>()                 // friendly format => Ok(500ms)
+"0.5s ago".parse::<SignedDuration>()       // friendly format => Ok(500ms)
+// but the sign survives when any integral component is non-zero:
+"-PT1.5S".parse::<Span>()                  // => Ok(-1s 500ms) correct
+```
+
+Why it's a library bug: `Span`'s `Display` prints `-1ns` as
+`"-PT0.000000001S"`, and `FromStr` documents parsing the ISO 8601 duration
+format, so print→parse must round-trip (the `temporal_parse.rs` fuzz target
+asserts exactly this fieldwise equality — but starting from *strings*, so it
+can only catch this if the fuzzer stumbles on a well-formed negative
+fraction-only duration string).
+
+Code path at fault: `crates/jiff/src/fmt/util.rs`.
+`DurationUnits::get_sign` (line ~686) returns `Sign::Zero` unless
+`any_non_zero_units` is set, and `any_non_zero_units` is only set by
+`set_unit_value` for *integral* unit values. `set_fraction` (line ~148)
+never marks a non-zero fraction. Consequently `get_fraction` (line ~672)
+sees a non-negative sign and returns the fraction un-negated. All duration
+parsers (ISO 8601 and "friendly", for both `Span` and `SignedDuration`)
+funnel through this type, which is why every format/type combination is
+affected.
+
+The failing test `span::tests::prop_display_fromstr_roundtrip_fieldwise`
+was left in place (failing), with a comment pointing here.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **`Zoned` Display round trip with second-precision fixed offsets.** First
+  version of `prop_display_fromstr_roundtrip` drew offsets at full second
+  precision. It failed: `TimeZone::fixed(+00:00:01)` prints as
+  `1970-01-01T00:00:01+00:00[+00:00]` — a *different instant* — and jiff
+  refuses to parse sub-minute offsets in this context. This is documented
+  behavior (`DateTimePrinter::print_time_zone`: RFC 3339/9557 don't support
+  sub-minute offsets, so printing a `Zoned` rounds the offset to the nearest
+  minute). The generator was constrained to whole-minute fixed offsets, with
+  a comment citing the doc. Not weakened otherwise.
+- Nothing else was dropped. The RFC 2822 test was *designed* constrained
+  (second precision, years 1–9999, whole-minute offsets) from the start
+  because those limits are documented properties of the format, not
+  discovered by failure.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Things that worked well: the workflow (evidence-gathering → port → new
+properties → run-and-reflect) mapped cleanly onto this crate; the fuzz
+directory pointer in SKILL.md was exactly the right evidence source; the
+MSRV gotcha in the Rust reference's Setup section predicted the first error
+I hit verbatim; `tc.reject()` made the quickcheck `TestResult::discard`
+ports mechanical; shrinking produced clean minimal counterexamples (`-1ns`,
+epoch timestamp).
+
+Issues, most important first — the first three are about the jiff extras
+module, which the task said had not been exercised before:
+
+1. **extras.md says nothing about the two-crates problem.** In the very
+   common case of testing jiff *itself* (or any crate that re-exports jiff
+   types from a git checkout), enabling hegeltest's `jiff` feature pulls a
+   second jiff from crates.io and every generator produces values of the
+   wrong crate instance. The errors are confusing ("expected `jiff::Span`,
+   found `jiff::Span`"). extras.md needs a paragraph: (a) add
+   `[patch.crates-io] <crate> = { path = ... }` when the crate under test
+   *is* the integration crate; (b) even then, inside the crate's own unit
+   tests the extras produce the *extern* crate's types, so tests must use
+   `jiff::...` paths, not `crate::...`, and tests needing `pub(crate)` APIs
+   can't use the extras at all. This applies equally to the chrono and
+   serde_json integrations.
+2. **`jiff_gs::spans()` is much narrower than documented.** extras.md just
+   says it yields `jiff::Span`, but it only generates single-unit
+   nanosecond spans (`Span::new().try_nanoseconds(n)`), never multi-unit or
+   calendar spans — and an i64 of nanoseconds only covers ±292 years of
+   jiff's ±9999-year span domain. Every interesting span property here
+   needed a hand-rolled composite (draw nanos, draw a largest `Unit`,
+   re-balance via `Span::round` relative to `0000-01-01`). Either the
+   generator should do this, or extras.md should document the limitation
+   and show that composite as a recipe.
+3. **The default `TimeZone`/`zoneds()` generator has no IANA zones.**
+   `zoneds()` samples UTC, `TimeZone::unknown()` and fixed offsets only, so
+   it can never exercise DST logic — arguably the whole point of jiff. The
+   task's "DST-aware arithmetic" properties required a hand-rolled
+   `sampled_from` over `TimeZone::get(...)` names. extras.md should say
+   this and suggest the pattern (plus interesting zones: Lord Howe,
+   Kathmandu, Apia). Also worth documenting: `zoneds()` with the default
+   timezone generator includes `TimeZone::unknown()`, which can surprise
+   round-trip properties.
+4. **Hegel's proc macros assume the `std` prelude.** In this `no_std +
+   alloc` crate, every `#[hegel::test]` failed with "cannot find macro
+   `format`" and "no method named `to_string` for `&'static str`" errors
+   pointing at the attribute, fixed by adding
+   `use alloc::{format, string::ToString};` to each tests module. The
+   reference's Gotchas section should mention this (jiff, chrono and many
+   other date/time crates are no_std-compatible, so the extras audience
+   will hit it disproportionately).
+5. **Minor, porting.md:** quickcheck's implicit size-biasing makes patterns
+   like "draw an arbitrary `i64`, discard unless in `[0, 86_400e9)`" work in
+   quickcheck but reject ~100% of cases under hegel's uniform draws. The
+   porting checklist covers "convert strategies", but an explicit warning
+   that `TestResult::discard` guards frequently must become generator
+   *bounds* (not `tc.assume`) would have saved a moment of thought on every
+   time.rs port.
+6. **Minor, reference.md:** a note that `#[cfg(not(miri))]` composes fine
+   with `#[hegel::test]` (put `cfg` first) would help when porting suites
+   that gate PBTs away from miri, as jiff does.
+
+Everything else — `#[hegel::composite]` (including parameterized ones),
+`sampled_from` over owned `TimeZone`/`Unit` values, `one_of`-free dependent
+draws, `test_cases` overrides, the failure database — worked exactly as
+documented.
